WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.462 --> 00:00:03.896
- I think it is very hard
to win in the long long term

00:00:03.896 --> 00:00:04.941
without the best product.

00:00:04.941 --> 00:00:08.073
Most of the founders that I
have spent a lot of time with

00:00:08.073 --> 00:00:09.834
that have gone on to be super successful

00:00:09.834 --> 00:00:12.585
spent a very long time on their idea

00:00:12.585 --> 00:00:14.688
when a lot of other people
would have given up.

00:00:14.688 --> 00:00:16.357
One of the things that
someone once told me

00:00:16.357 --> 00:00:18.439
was most impressive about Mark Zuckerberg,

00:00:18.439 --> 00:00:20.043
is not that he hired pretty well

00:00:20.043 --> 00:00:21.986
because a lot of founders do that,

00:00:21.986 --> 00:00:23.647
but that he fired well.

00:00:23.647 --> 00:00:26.253
And he could fire people quickly
that weren't working out.

00:00:26.253 --> 00:00:28.353
- He's an American
entrepreneur, programmer,

00:00:28.353 --> 00:00:30.284
venture capitalist, and blogger.

00:00:30.284 --> 00:00:32.500
He's the president of Y Combinator,

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:34.623
he's a personal investor
into many companies

00:00:34.623 --> 00:00:37.376
such as Airbnb, Stripe, and Pinterest.

00:00:37.376 --> 00:00:39.280
He's Sam Altman and here's my take

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:41.167
on his top 10 rules of success.

00:00:41.167 --> 00:00:42.767
Rule number one is my personal favorite

00:00:42.767 --> 00:00:45.172
and I'd love to know which
one you guys like the best.

00:00:45.172 --> 00:00:47.480
And as always guys, as
you're watching the videos,

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:49.281
if you hear something that
really resonates with you

00:00:49.281 --> 00:00:51.286
please leave it down the comments below

00:00:51.286 --> 00:00:52.531
and put quotes around it

00:00:52.531 --> 00:00:54.020
so other people can be inspired as well.

00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:56.049
Also as you are writing something down

00:00:56.049 --> 00:01:00.216
it's much more likely to
stick in your head too, enjoy!

00:01:03.359 --> 00:01:05.942
(upbeat music)

00:01:13.199 --> 00:01:15.208
- Yeah, I think if you
don't actually believe

00:01:15.208 --> 00:01:16.593
what you're doing is really important,

00:01:16.593 --> 00:01:20.476
if you don't derive satisfaction
from what you're doing,

00:01:20.476 --> 00:01:24.525
then you will not be able to
sustain all of the bad things

00:01:24.525 --> 00:01:26.635
that happen in the incredibly
long period of time

00:01:26.635 --> 00:01:28.408
that the bad things happen over.

00:01:28.408 --> 00:01:31.951
And so the only motivation that
I have seen work for people

00:01:31.951 --> 00:01:34.060
over a long period of time

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:36.976
is enjoyment in what they're doing

00:01:36.976 --> 00:01:38.746
and an intense belief that it matters

00:01:38.746 --> 00:01:40.118
and ideally liking the people

00:01:40.118 --> 00:01:42.013
that they go to work with every day.

00:01:42.013 --> 00:01:46.016
But the, I think the reasons
that a lot of people,

00:01:46.016 --> 00:01:47.272
and by the way it's totally cool

00:01:47.272 --> 00:01:48.707
when people start off saying

00:01:48.707 --> 00:01:50.457
"Well, I want to make money
or I want to be famous."

00:01:50.457 --> 00:01:51.740
I think a lot of people start that way

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:53.089
and they don't like to admit it.

00:01:53.089 --> 00:01:56.248
But pretty quickly, or at
least in the first few years,

00:01:56.248 --> 00:01:59.236
I think a lot of people
find a deeper mission

00:01:59.236 --> 00:02:00.844
for why they do what they do

00:02:00.844 --> 00:02:01.732
and then that drives them

00:02:01.732 --> 00:02:04.401
for the rest of the time they work.

00:02:04.401 --> 00:02:06.450
But I think if you'll look at

00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:08.008
the companies that endure over decades,

00:02:08.008 --> 00:02:10.668
the companies that endure over decades,

00:02:10.668 --> 00:02:11.892
they tend to win on product.

00:02:11.892 --> 00:02:13.454
they tend to win on product.

00:02:13.454 --> 00:02:15.159
It is true that it is critical

00:02:15.159 --> 00:02:17.031
to get sales and marketing right

00:02:17.031 --> 00:02:19.862
because without that you just
don't get the flywheel going

00:02:19.862 --> 00:02:23.362
but the most valuable tech
company in the world is Apple

00:02:23.362 --> 00:02:24.962
but the most valuable tech
company in the world is Apple

00:02:24.962 --> 00:02:26.672
and I think they make

00:02:26.672 --> 00:02:29.680
just the best products across the board.

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:31.519
I don't know if Google is still number two

00:02:31.519 --> 00:02:34.457
but I think Google Search
dominates everything else

00:02:34.457 --> 00:02:35.290
and also like Gmail and Google Maps

00:02:35.290 --> 00:02:38.191
and also like Gmail and Google Maps

00:02:38.191 --> 00:02:40.099
dominate their respective products.

00:02:40.099 --> 00:02:42.232
I think Airbnb won on product.

00:02:42.232 --> 00:02:45.208
I think that Facebook, although
they came late to the game

00:02:45.208 --> 00:02:47.544
had the best product by far

00:02:47.544 --> 00:02:49.720
and they've managed to expand that.

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:50.920
So I think, I don't ever try

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:52.953
So I think, I don't ever try

00:02:52.953 --> 00:02:55.371
to let people off the hook
for sales and marketing

00:02:55.371 --> 00:02:57.218
'cause if you build the
best product in the world

00:02:57.218 --> 00:03:00.784
and don't get anyone to use
it that still kind of sucks

00:03:00.784 --> 00:03:04.660
but I think it is very hard
to win in the long long term

00:03:04.660 --> 00:03:06.177
but I think it is very hard
to win in the long long term

00:03:06.177 --> 00:03:07.551
without the best product.

00:03:07.551 --> 00:03:09.826
I think you can get acquired two years in

00:03:09.826 --> 00:03:13.445
because you had a really good
press or marketing strategy

00:03:13.445 --> 00:03:15.757
but that does not,

00:03:15.757 --> 00:03:18.453
that is not how you create enduring value.

00:03:18.453 --> 00:03:21.168
Eventually, these things converge.

00:03:21.168 --> 00:03:23.917
Ignore people telling
you your idea is bad.

00:03:23.917 --> 00:03:26.089
They may be right but the only way to know

00:03:26.089 --> 00:03:27.776
is to actually build it out.

00:03:27.776 --> 00:03:31.346
And I think the thing that
works about Silicon Valley

00:03:31.346 --> 00:03:33.142
that's different in Mexico City

00:03:33.142 --> 00:03:35.081
or almost anywhere else in the world

00:03:35.081 --> 00:03:37.069
is the default response.

00:03:37.069 --> 00:03:38.190
If someone says,

00:03:38.190 --> 00:03:40.370
"Hey I'm going to start a
startup, here's my idea."

00:03:40.370 --> 00:03:43.361
The default response is to
be excited and think like

00:03:43.361 --> 00:03:45.011
"Oh, why might that work."

00:03:45.011 --> 00:03:47.292
And in most of the rest of the world

00:03:47.292 --> 00:03:49.501
the default response when
you tell someone your idea

00:03:49.501 --> 00:03:51.733
is to say, "Oh that's not going to work."

00:03:51.733 --> 00:03:54.361
And this is really toxic for startups

00:03:54.361 --> 00:03:56.531
'cause you have to believe in yourself.

00:03:56.531 --> 00:03:59.509
And the good ideas, because
they sound like bad ideas,

00:03:59.509 --> 00:04:01.622
are so fragile and so killable

00:04:01.622 --> 00:04:04.539
that you have to be really careful

00:04:04.539 --> 00:04:06.380
to insulate yourself against this.

00:04:06.380 --> 00:04:07.213
Knowing when to quit

00:04:07.213 --> 00:04:08.441
Knowing when to quit

00:04:08.441 --> 00:04:11.727
and knowing when to
give up on something is,

00:04:11.727 --> 00:04:14.189
there's no perfect answer to that.

00:04:14.189 --> 00:04:15.443
But it's really challenging

00:04:15.443 --> 00:04:17.381
to even get that approximately correct.

00:04:17.381 --> 00:04:20.389
I think most people give
up on things way too early.

00:04:20.389 --> 00:04:22.427
So the mistake that most people make

00:04:22.427 --> 00:04:24.174
is they try something,

00:04:24.174 --> 00:04:25.359
it does not immediately work.

00:04:25.359 --> 00:04:27.286
You see this particularly
in young entrepreneurs.

00:04:27.286 --> 00:04:28.670
It does not immediately work.

00:04:28.670 --> 00:04:29.985
After seven weeks they say,

00:04:29.985 --> 00:04:31.377
"You know what I tried this thing,

00:04:31.377 --> 00:04:32.723
"it's just not meant to be

00:04:32.723 --> 00:04:34.256
"and I have too many other projects."

00:04:34.256 --> 00:04:35.529
So they immediately give up

00:04:35.529 --> 00:04:36.918
and you kind of,

00:04:36.918 --> 00:04:38.385
the satirical version of this

00:04:38.385 --> 00:04:41.601
is people that are 23 and
have started 14 startups

00:04:41.601 --> 00:04:43.277
because they give up on everyone

00:04:43.277 --> 00:04:45.073
before they're ever possibly successful.

00:04:45.073 --> 00:04:46.894
These things are really hard.

00:04:46.894 --> 00:04:48.277
They take a very long time.

00:04:48.277 --> 00:04:49.271
There are a lot of critics.

00:04:49.271 --> 00:04:51.184
There are a lot of people
who say this thing sucks.

00:04:51.184 --> 00:04:52.041
It's going to fail.

00:04:52.041 --> 00:04:53.111
It's really stupid.

00:04:53.111 --> 00:04:55.426
And then also there's what at YC

00:04:55.426 --> 00:04:56.860
we call the "Trough of Sorrow,"

00:04:56.860 --> 00:04:59.281
where no one even bothers to say it sucks

00:04:59.281 --> 00:05:01.646
because no one cares at all.

00:05:01.646 --> 00:05:04.729
And that is at least as demotivating.

00:05:05.770 --> 00:05:08.830
Most of the founders that I
have spent a lot of time with

00:05:08.830 --> 00:05:10.715
that have gone on to be super successful

00:05:10.715 --> 00:05:13.453
spent a very long time on their idea

00:05:13.453 --> 00:05:15.983
when a lot of other people
would have given up.

00:05:15.983 --> 00:05:17.441
And either people said it sucks

00:05:17.441 --> 00:05:19.117
or people said nothing at all.

00:05:19.117 --> 00:05:22.426
And a framework that I
have for when to give up

00:05:22.426 --> 00:05:23.602
and when to keep working

00:05:23.602 --> 00:05:27.170
is it should be an internal,
not an external decision.

00:05:27.170 --> 00:05:30.721
If people aren't using it or
people are saying it's bad,

00:05:30.721 --> 00:05:32.245
that alone is not a reason to give up.

00:05:32.245 --> 00:05:33.901
You want to pay some attention to that.

00:05:33.901 --> 00:05:35.127
They might be right.

00:05:35.127 --> 00:05:38.103
But I think the best entrepreneurs I know,

00:05:38.103 --> 00:05:39.494
But I think the best entrepreneurs I know,

00:05:39.494 --> 00:05:42.056
they make an internal
decision about when to give up

00:05:42.056 --> 00:05:43.616
or when to keep working on something.

00:05:43.616 --> 00:05:46.704
It's basically when you
have run out of ideas

00:05:46.704 --> 00:05:49.657
and something is not working,
then it's a good time to stop.

00:05:49.657 --> 00:05:50.724
Startups, to be successful,

00:05:50.724 --> 00:05:52.589
have to build something that people want.

00:05:52.589 --> 00:05:53.914
If you get that right,

00:05:53.914 --> 00:05:56.575
you can get everything
else wrong, pretty much.

00:05:56.575 --> 00:05:58.055
If you get that wrong,

00:05:58.055 --> 00:05:59.695
you can get everything else perfect

00:05:59.695 --> 00:06:01.007
and we see this all the time.

00:06:01.007 --> 00:06:03.682
We see startups that hire the
best lawyer in the valley.

00:06:03.682 --> 00:06:05.064
They get a beautiful office.

00:06:05.064 --> 00:06:08.613
They work with really great
accountants and a great PR firm.

00:06:08.613 --> 00:06:12.030
They do everything that
a startup needs to do,

00:06:12.030 --> 00:06:16.712
except build a product that
customers or users want

00:06:16.712 --> 00:06:18.793
and this is so important.

00:06:18.793 --> 00:06:21.849
This is probably the most
important piece of startup advice

00:06:21.849 --> 00:06:23.902
in the early days.

00:06:23.902 --> 00:06:26.147
This is the only thing that
matters, you have to do this.

00:06:26.147 --> 00:06:28.058
You can ignore all the other advice.

00:06:28.058 --> 00:06:31.029
You can ignore all of the
crazy stuff that people do

00:06:31.029 --> 00:06:32.507
when they're trying to advise you

00:06:32.507 --> 00:06:33.944
about how to set up a company.

00:06:33.944 --> 00:06:36.416
In fact, you should ignore most advice.

00:06:36.416 --> 00:06:38.262
Most startup advice is terrible.

00:06:38.262 --> 00:06:40.315
You should feel free to
ignore any of this too,

00:06:40.315 --> 00:06:41.806
except this one thing.

00:06:41.806 --> 00:06:44.514
If your startup doesn't make
something that users want,

00:06:44.514 --> 00:06:46.918
the startup will never ever be successful.

00:06:46.918 --> 00:06:50.207
I think, in general, you really should try

00:06:50.207 --> 00:06:51.887
to live your life without regrets

00:06:51.887 --> 00:06:53.933
and if you are really set

00:06:53.933 --> 00:06:55.747
on starting a particular company,

00:06:55.747 --> 00:06:58.154
even if you know that you could go

00:06:58.154 --> 00:07:00.168
be employee number 30 at Airbnb

00:07:00.168 --> 00:07:02.962
and Airbnb is already going like this

00:07:02.962 --> 00:07:05.114
and you'd have more of an impact

00:07:05.114 --> 00:07:07.567
but if you're going to regret
for the rest of your life

00:07:07.567 --> 00:07:09.145
not starting this particular company

00:07:09.145 --> 00:07:10.576
'cause you're so in love with it.

00:07:10.576 --> 00:07:12.055
You might decide to do that.

00:07:12.055 --> 00:07:15.082
However, I think this other thing like

00:07:15.082 --> 00:07:17.060
making an impact on whatever you do

00:07:17.060 --> 00:07:18.741
making an impact on whatever you do

00:07:18.741 --> 00:07:22.363
is the satisfying thing in
life, in professional life.

00:07:22.363 --> 00:07:24.795
And so I think most people find that

00:07:24.795 --> 00:07:26.889
their job satisfaction goes up

00:07:26.889 --> 00:07:29.181
with the impact they have on their company

00:07:29.181 --> 00:07:30.834
or the world or whatever.

00:07:30.834 --> 00:07:34.286
And if you start a company
that is a mild success

00:07:34.286 --> 00:07:36.898
And if you start a company
that is a mild success

00:07:36.898 --> 00:07:39.319
or join a company that's a huge success,

00:07:39.319 --> 00:07:42.338
you probably have more of an
impact in the latter case.

00:07:42.338 --> 00:07:45.039
I think people have terrible
risk calculus in general.

00:07:45.039 --> 00:07:48.015
Even people who try to
be really good about this

00:07:48.015 --> 00:07:49.084
are bad at it.

00:07:49.084 --> 00:07:51.425
The answer, I think, is almost always

00:07:51.425 --> 00:07:55.492
A, you're wrong about what is
risky and what is not risky,

00:07:55.492 --> 00:07:58.865
and B, most people don't take enough risk.

00:07:58.865 --> 00:08:00.504
Especially early in your career.

00:08:00.504 --> 00:08:01.762
Being young and unknown and poor,

00:08:01.762 --> 00:08:03.756
Being young and unknown and poor,

00:08:03.756 --> 00:08:05.599
that is actually a great gift

00:08:05.599 --> 00:08:07.862
in terms of the amount
of risk you can take

00:08:07.862 --> 00:08:11.264
and I think people don't
capitalize on that enough.

00:08:11.264 --> 00:08:13.271
I think what risk actually looks like

00:08:13.271 --> 00:08:16.121
is not doing something that you will then

00:08:16.121 --> 00:08:19.268
spend the rest of your life regretting.

00:08:19.268 --> 00:08:20.242
And there's a quote,

00:08:20.242 --> 00:08:22.284
"People regret way more
the things they didn't do

00:08:22.284 --> 00:08:23.815
"than things that they did do."

00:08:23.815 --> 00:08:25.495
So if you really believe in something

00:08:25.495 --> 00:08:27.547
and there's an idea that
you're super passionate about

00:08:27.547 --> 00:08:31.843
and you take a calculated
risk to start a company,

00:08:31.843 --> 00:08:35.287
realizing you may forgo a couple
of years of steady income,

00:08:35.287 --> 00:08:36.120
another job, and whatever else

00:08:36.120 --> 00:08:38.521
another job, and whatever else

00:08:38.521 --> 00:08:42.833
and maybe people call you
a failure or whatever,

00:08:42.833 --> 00:08:44.312
that's a great risk to take.

00:08:44.312 --> 00:08:45.567
And if you don't take that risk,

00:08:45.567 --> 00:08:47.762
I think you have a very high chance

00:08:47.762 --> 00:08:50.043
that you'll end up regretting that.

00:08:50.043 --> 00:08:52.563
I think the wrong kind of risk to take

00:08:52.563 --> 00:08:54.054
I think the wrong kind of risk to take

00:08:54.054 --> 00:08:57.082
is where people don't actually do things

00:08:57.082 --> 00:08:59.221
or don't commit to something
because they don't want to fail

00:08:59.221 --> 00:09:00.600
and they overrate the risk of failure

00:09:00.600 --> 00:09:04.094
and the reputation damage or
embarrassment or whatever.

00:09:04.094 --> 00:09:04.927
I think one really important
thing to strive for

00:09:04.927 --> 00:09:08.146
I think one really important
thing to strive for

00:09:08.146 --> 00:09:11.896
in your career is to be
a doer, not a talker.

00:09:12.954 --> 00:09:16.412
And the reason that people don't do stuff,

00:09:16.412 --> 00:09:19.242
one, is it's hard and two, is it's risky.

00:09:19.242 --> 00:09:20.812
And so you have these people

00:09:20.812 --> 00:09:23.633
that want to dabble in a
bunch of different projects,

00:09:23.633 --> 00:09:25.636
but never be all-in on one.

00:09:25.636 --> 00:09:28.491
And I think it's this
combination of work and risk

00:09:28.491 --> 00:09:31.242
and it's, I think that's really bad.

00:09:31.242 --> 00:09:32.972
I think history belongs to the doers

00:09:32.972 --> 00:09:36.282
and I think you should take a
risk, actually do something.

00:09:36.282 --> 00:09:37.770
Don't just sit around
and talk and organize

00:09:37.770 --> 00:09:38.791
and bring together groups

00:09:38.791 --> 00:09:40.926
and go to thought-leadership conferences

00:09:40.926 --> 00:09:43.782
and whatever it is these
people spend their time on

00:09:43.782 --> 00:09:46.679
other than actually building something

00:09:46.679 --> 00:09:49.059
and just take the risk of committing

00:09:49.059 --> 00:09:51.042
to what you have conviction on.

00:09:51.042 --> 00:09:53.625
Team problems are the
biggest killer of startups

00:09:53.625 --> 00:09:55.398
inside of YC.

00:09:55.398 --> 00:09:56.982
Everyone thinks that startups die

00:09:56.982 --> 00:09:58.768
at the hands of competitors

00:09:58.768 --> 00:10:00.950
but startups commit suicide

00:10:00.950 --> 00:10:04.798
much more often than startups
get killed by somebody else.

00:10:04.798 --> 00:10:06.747
If you don't have a good team,

00:10:06.747 --> 00:10:09.269
eventually the startup will implode.

00:10:09.269 --> 00:10:11.094
And if you do have a great team,

00:10:11.094 --> 00:10:14.163
the startup will just go on to
do better and better things.

00:10:14.163 --> 00:10:17.193
So there are a number
of jobs that a CEO has

00:10:17.193 --> 00:10:20.405
but hiring I think is the most important.

00:10:20.405 --> 00:10:22.805
If you don't hire really well,

00:10:22.805 --> 00:10:25.204
your company will never be a big success.

00:10:25.204 --> 00:10:28.121
As your company gets bigger and bigger,

00:10:28.121 --> 00:10:30.401
the only thing the CEO gets to decide on

00:10:30.401 --> 00:10:33.171
is the company strategy
and who the players are

00:10:33.171 --> 00:10:34.277
who the people are.

00:10:34.277 --> 00:10:36.680
Everything else gets done by other people.

00:10:36.680 --> 00:10:38.359
So you have to hire incredibly well.

00:10:38.359 --> 00:10:41.732
You have to spend a third
of your time hiring people

00:10:41.732 --> 00:10:44.269
and recruiting the best
people in the world.

00:10:44.269 --> 00:10:46.830
Even still at YC, this is
the biggest part of my job.

00:10:46.830 --> 00:10:48.450
I can't advise every startup

00:10:48.450 --> 00:10:51.495
but I can make sure we got the
best partners in the world.

00:10:51.495 --> 00:10:54.293
And then if you make a mistake hiring

00:10:54.293 --> 00:10:56.140
you have to fix it incredibly quickly.

00:10:56.140 --> 00:10:57.971
Most founders understand

00:10:57.971 --> 00:10:59.960
that it's important to hire really well

00:10:59.960 --> 00:11:02.536
and most founders prioritize that.

00:11:02.536 --> 00:11:04.753
But it's also important
to fire people quickly

00:11:04.753 --> 00:11:07.645
that aren't A members of the team.

00:11:07.645 --> 00:11:10.987
And most founders don't understand this.

00:11:10.987 --> 00:11:12.534
Or they understand it but it's so painful

00:11:12.534 --> 00:11:15.143
they don't want to do it.

00:11:15.143 --> 00:11:17.104
One of the things that
someone once told me

00:11:17.104 --> 00:11:19.346
was most impressive about Mark Zuckerberg,

00:11:19.346 --> 00:11:21.340
is not that he hired pretty well

00:11:21.340 --> 00:11:22.948
because a lot of founders do that,

00:11:22.948 --> 00:11:24.090
but that he fired well.

00:11:24.090 --> 00:11:26.922
And he could fire people quickly
that weren't working out.

00:11:26.922 --> 00:11:29.787
And this is really hard to
learn how to do as a founder.

00:11:29.787 --> 00:11:33.394
Losing focus is another way
that founders get off track.

00:11:33.394 --> 00:11:34.696
Losing focus is another way
that founders get off track.

00:11:34.696 --> 00:11:37.371
I actually think that this
is a symptom of burnout.

00:11:37.371 --> 00:11:39.411
When you get really burned
out on running business

00:11:39.411 --> 00:11:42.973
you want to do easier things
or more gratifying things.

00:11:42.973 --> 00:11:45.010
You want to go to conferences

00:11:45.010 --> 00:11:46.933
and have people tell
you how great you are.

00:11:46.933 --> 00:11:48.206
You want to do all these things

00:11:48.206 --> 00:11:49.982
that are not actually building a business.

00:11:49.982 --> 00:11:51.098
And the most common post-YC failure case

00:11:51.098 --> 00:11:54.362
And the most common post-YC failure case

00:11:54.362 --> 00:11:55.840
for the companies we fund

00:11:55.840 --> 00:11:57.182
is that they're incredible focused

00:11:57.182 --> 00:11:58.959
during YC on their company

00:11:58.959 --> 00:12:01.394
and then after, they start
doing a lot of other things.

00:12:01.394 --> 00:12:05.561
They advise companies, they
go to conferences, whatever.

00:12:06.433 --> 00:12:09.626
Focus is what made you
successful in the first place.

00:12:09.626 --> 00:12:11.855
There are a lot of
reasons people lose focus.

00:12:11.855 --> 00:12:13.996
But fight against that
really, really hard.

00:12:13.996 --> 00:12:16.406
Here is one really important point,

00:12:16.406 --> 00:12:20.055
which is that people get
more ambitious over time.

00:12:20.055 --> 00:12:24.400
And when the founders of
Airbnb first came to us,

00:12:24.400 --> 00:12:27.194
they were delighted they had three users.

00:12:27.194 --> 00:12:28.387
And if we told them that someday

00:12:28.387 --> 00:12:30.845
they were going to be running
a 50 billion dollar company,

00:12:30.845 --> 00:12:31.824
whatever it's going to be,

00:12:31.824 --> 00:12:34.765
with more might than any
hotel chain in the world,

00:12:34.765 --> 00:12:36.891
they would have looked at us and said

00:12:36.891 --> 00:12:39.273
"You're crazy, we don't
want to work with you

00:12:39.273 --> 00:12:41.306
'cause you don't know what
you're talking about."

00:12:41.306 --> 00:12:45.140
So I think there's this pressure
in the last couple of years

00:12:45.140 --> 00:12:47.141
to have an immediate vision

00:12:47.141 --> 00:12:48.536
about how you're going to have

00:12:48.536 --> 00:12:50.914
this 10, 100 billion dollar company,

00:12:50.914 --> 00:12:55.082
and no one honestly has
that in their first days.

00:12:55.082 --> 00:12:58.361
Google almost sold the
company for a million dollars.

00:12:58.361 --> 00:13:01.861
They would have if the offer came through.

00:13:06.358 --> 00:13:09.519
Zuckerberg very very nearly sold Facebook

00:13:09.519 --> 00:13:10.921
for a billion dollars.

00:13:10.921 --> 00:13:14.498
So it's okay to become
more ambitious as you go.

00:13:14.498 --> 00:13:17.187
So it's okay to become
more ambitious as you go.

00:13:17.187 --> 00:13:19.191
In fact, that's what should happen.

00:13:19.191 --> 00:13:20.760
So what we look for

00:13:20.760 --> 00:13:23.976
is people that have a kernel of an idea

00:13:23.976 --> 00:13:26.003
that we believe can develop
into something huge.

00:13:26.003 --> 00:13:27.638
But you can't focus on that right away.

00:13:27.638 --> 00:13:30.456
You have to focus on
delivering something great

00:13:30.456 --> 00:13:32.618
for a small number of users first.

00:13:32.618 --> 00:13:33.959
The way we talk about this

00:13:33.959 --> 00:13:37.969
is that you can either
create an initial product

00:13:37.969 --> 00:13:39.254
that a lot of users really like

00:13:39.254 --> 00:13:41.854
or that a small number
of users really love.

00:13:41.854 --> 00:13:43.176
Obviously you'd like to make something

00:13:43.176 --> 00:13:44.933
that a lot of users really love

00:13:44.933 --> 00:13:46.593
but those ideas get taken by big companies

00:13:46.593 --> 00:13:47.809
and we can't compete with them.

00:13:47.809 --> 00:13:49.334
So you don't have to have

00:13:49.334 --> 00:13:51.563
a take over the world plan on day one.

00:13:51.563 --> 00:13:53.149
In fact, very few of the companies

00:13:53.149 --> 00:13:54.830
that we've been involved with

00:13:54.830 --> 00:13:55.955
that have gone on to do it

00:13:55.955 --> 00:13:58.271
felt they were going to initially.

00:13:58.271 --> 00:14:00.506
They just, the founder had
something really passionate about

00:14:00.506 --> 00:14:02.406
they had a plan, as Scott was saying,

00:14:02.406 --> 00:14:04.907
about how they had some defensibility,

00:14:04.907 --> 00:14:08.990
and it just grows and
grows and grows from there.

00:14:10.089 --> 00:14:11.572
- Thank you guys so much for watching!

00:14:11.572 --> 00:14:13.940
I made this video because
Kevin asked me to.

00:14:13.940 --> 00:14:15.256
So if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:14:15.256 --> 00:14:16.553
that you want me to profile next

00:14:16.553 --> 00:14:18.902
leave it down the comments below
and I'll see what I can do.

00:14:18.902 --> 00:14:20.636
I'd also love to know what did Sam say

00:14:20.636 --> 00:14:22.928
that had the biggest
impact on you and why?

00:14:22.928 --> 00:14:24.560
What are you going to take from this video

00:14:24.560 --> 00:14:26.801
and immediately apply it somehow

00:14:26.801 --> 00:14:28.264
to your life or to your business?

00:14:28.264 --> 00:14:29.436
Leave it down the comments below

00:14:29.436 --> 00:14:31.060
and I'm going to join in the discussion.

00:14:31.060 --> 00:14:33.328
I also want to give a quick
shout out to Amalia's Design.

00:14:33.328 --> 00:14:34.586
Thank you so much for picking up

00:14:34.586 --> 00:14:36.291
a copy of my book, Your One Word,

00:14:36.291 --> 00:14:38.743
and making that tribute
picture to it as well.

00:14:38.743 --> 00:14:40.472
I really really appreciate your support.

00:14:40.472 --> 00:14:42.235
So thank you guys again for watching.

00:14:42.235 --> 00:14:43.268
I believe in you.

00:14:43.268 --> 00:14:45.201
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:14:45.201 --> 00:14:47.874
and whatever your one word is, much love.

00:14:47.874 --> 00:14:49.374
I'll see you soon.

00:14:54.679 --> 00:14:57.236
- Yeah, and I think
people also seem to have

00:14:57.236 --> 00:15:00.146
sort of the fear of being told no a lot

00:15:00.146 --> 00:15:01.781
and not asking for what they want.

00:15:01.781 --> 00:15:02.614
I'm sure that's something

00:15:02.614 --> 00:15:04.351
that you've encountered with people.

00:15:04.351 --> 00:15:05.184
- Yeah, this was one of
the other things I learned

00:15:05.184 --> 00:15:09.104
- Yeah, this was one of
the other things I learned

00:15:09.104 --> 00:15:12.568
that I wish I had gotten advice
on early on in my career,

00:15:12.568 --> 00:15:14.644
is ask for what you want.

00:15:14.644 --> 00:15:17.724
You will get told no a lot,
but sometimes it will work.

00:15:17.724 --> 00:15:20.317
And I think you see a lot of entrepreneurs

00:15:20.317 --> 00:15:22.043
shoot themselves in the foot

00:15:22.043 --> 00:15:22.876
because they don't ask that person

00:15:22.876 --> 00:15:25.015
because they don't ask that person

00:15:25.015 --> 00:15:26.671
to quit their job and come join them,

00:15:26.671 --> 00:15:30.014
they don't ask this big
company to do a deal with them

00:15:30.014 --> 00:15:32.361
and they're just not aggressive enough.

00:15:32.361 --> 00:15:36.128
And I think you being willing
to ask for what you want

00:15:36.128 --> 00:15:37.803
And I think you being willing
to ask for what you want

00:15:37.803 --> 00:15:39.184
and be somewhat aggressive

00:15:39.184 --> 00:15:41.540
are really important characteristics

00:15:41.540 --> 00:15:42.797
of being an entrepreneur.

00:15:42.797 --> 00:15:44.801
People don't want to fail.

00:15:44.801 --> 00:15:47.004
They don't want to be told no.

00:15:47.004 --> 00:15:49.363
They don't want to end up in some crisis.

00:15:49.363 --> 00:15:52.440
How to keep track productivity systems.

00:15:52.440 --> 00:15:54.331
So, the one I use which I
actually thinks works really well

00:15:54.331 --> 00:15:57.170
So, the one I use which I
actually thinks works really well

00:15:57.170 --> 00:16:00.374
is I keep one piece of paper with my goals

00:16:00.374 --> 00:16:02.128
is I keep one piece of paper with my goals

00:16:02.128 --> 00:16:04.283
for sort of a three to 12 month timeframe.

00:16:04.283 --> 00:16:05.674
And I look at that every day.

00:16:05.674 --> 00:16:07.917
And then separately I keep one page

00:16:07.917 --> 00:16:10.749
for every day of my
short-term goals for that day.

00:16:10.749 --> 00:16:12.578
And so if I need to do
something in like a week

00:16:12.578 --> 00:16:14.198
I just flip forward seven
pages and I write down.

00:16:14.198 --> 00:16:17.008
And then I also keep
a list of every person

00:16:17.008 --> 00:16:19.590
and what they're working on
and what I need to tell them

00:16:19.590 --> 00:16:20.423
and what I need to talk to them about.

00:16:20.423 --> 00:16:21.751
What we talked about last time.

00:16:21.751 --> 00:16:22.882
So every time I sit down with someone

00:16:22.882 --> 00:16:24.141
I kind of have the full state

00:16:24.141 --> 00:16:27.202
and a list of things for that
person that works really well.

00:16:27.202 --> 00:16:28.160
I think that there are,

00:16:28.160 --> 00:16:30.604
in addition to the interpersonal pieces,

00:16:30.604 --> 00:16:34.102
there are two other things
that we really think about

00:16:34.102 --> 00:16:36.368
that make a culture great,

00:16:36.368 --> 00:16:37.711
one is momentum.

00:16:37.711 --> 00:16:39.502
So yeah, winning teams
tend to keep winning

00:16:39.502 --> 00:16:41.598
and teams that take their eye off the ball

00:16:41.598 --> 00:16:43.514
and start losing, tend to keep losing.

00:16:43.514 --> 00:16:46.048
And so one of the most important
jobs as the entrepreneur

00:16:46.048 --> 00:16:48.531
is to make sure the
company never stops winning

00:16:48.531 --> 00:16:51.411
because it's very difficult
to dig yourself out of a hole

00:16:51.411 --> 00:16:52.692
and if you keep that going,

00:16:52.692 --> 00:16:55.016
it's this sort of, this is ongoing.

00:16:55.016 --> 00:16:57.227
It's this ongoing tail end.

00:16:57.227 --> 00:16:59.799
The other thing that we see as sort of

00:16:59.799 --> 00:17:02.675
the missionary and mercenary divide.

00:17:02.675 --> 00:17:04.829
I think most cultures end up

00:17:04.829 --> 00:17:08.221
being either a mercenary
culture or a missionary culture.

00:17:08.221 --> 00:17:11.284
And the great companies are
always the missionary cultures.

00:17:11.284 --> 00:17:12.686
If people believe in mission,

00:17:12.686 --> 00:17:14.079
if you are sufficiently ambitious

00:17:14.079 --> 00:17:15.366
if you present people,

00:17:15.366 --> 00:17:17.082
"We're going to make this big impact

00:17:17.082 --> 00:17:18.725
"and we're going to reach these people

00:17:18.725 --> 00:17:19.980
"and we're going to do
this important thing.

00:17:19.980 --> 00:17:21.971
"It's going to be great for the world."

00:17:21.971 --> 00:17:23.794
That's what motivates the best people.

00:17:23.794 --> 00:17:25.406
And if you have a culture like that

00:17:25.406 --> 00:17:29.150
where you do have this
ambitious important thing.

00:17:29.150 --> 00:17:30.543
It's easy to motivate people.

00:17:30.543 --> 00:17:33.870
In many ways, it is easier
to start a hard company

00:17:33.870 --> 00:17:35.663
than it is to start an easy company.

00:17:35.663 --> 00:17:37.429
More people will be excited,

00:17:37.429 --> 00:17:38.410
will have a better culture

00:17:38.410 --> 00:17:39.623
if you're doing something that matters,

00:17:39.623 --> 00:17:42.651
if you're doing this big thing.

00:17:42.651 --> 00:17:44.285
If you have the kind of culture

00:17:44.285 --> 00:17:45.591
where you're telling people,

00:17:45.591 --> 00:17:47.669
"You need to get into your
desk at nine and leave at nine

00:17:47.669 --> 00:17:49.067
"'cause we work 12-hour days."

00:17:49.067 --> 00:17:51.586
People might do that for a little while

00:17:51.586 --> 00:17:53.608
but that is almost never,

00:17:53.608 --> 00:17:54.591
in fact that's just,

00:17:54.591 --> 00:17:57.448
that is not what I have ever
seen produce great results.

